sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch09-01> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The extinct intermediate forms required by descent with modification link each species to a common, usually unknown, progenitor, and that progenitor will generally have differed in some respects from all of its modified descendants, so forms directly intermediate between two living species should not be expected.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Extinct intermediates connect each species to a common progenitor, not to each other";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IX, “On the Imperfection of the Geological Record”; section “On the nature of extinct intermediate varieties; on their number”. Scope: all pairs of related species, living or extinct"
}
